  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1226 AM CST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\nQuiet & mild weather continues today with temepratures again\napproaching 60 degrees this afternoon. Clouds will be \nincreasing through the afternoon as a pair of upper level \ndisturbances approach the Upper Midwest this evening through \nWednesday. The stronger of these arrives tonight, and is \nexpected to generate a swath of light rain across northern & \ncentral Minnesota into western Wisconsin. rainfall amounts will\nbe light, on the order of 0.1\" or less. The second moves over \nthe area Wednesday & brings a string cold front through the \nregion along with falling temperatures late Wednesday afternoon \ninto Thursday morning. In fact, it's a possible some spots could\nsee their first snowflakes of the season early Thursday morning\nif the cyclonic flow aloft behind the front is strong enough to\ngenerate some isolated showers.\n\nA much cooler airmass settles over the region Thursday into\nthe weekend as Canadian high pressure persists over the Upper\nMidwest. Highs Thursday through Sunday are only expected to \nreach the mid 30s to low 40s with overnight lows falling well \ninto the 20s. Typical temperatures for mid-November are the low \nto mid 40s for highs & around 30 for lows, & we have to go back\nto mid- October to find our last stretch of below-normal \ntemperatures. No precipitation is expected through the weekend\nwit dry high pressure overhead, with our next chance at\nwidespread precipitation likely not arriving until the middle of\nnext week.",
